Looking at the diff: in `parseYamlInteger` (the YAML 1.1 `!!int` resolver), the sign-handling block was replaced with `if (false)`, so `value` keeps its `-`/`+` prefix and `sign` is always `1`.

The pattern `YAML_INTEGER_PATTERN` explicitly allows signed forms (`[-+]?0b…`, `[-+]?0[0-7_]…`, `[-+]?0x…`, `[-+]?[0-9]…(:…)`), so signed values do reach this parser. Consequences on the patched build (observable via `load(..., { schema: YAML11_SCHEMA })` and `DUMP_SCHEMA`):

- `-010`: no longer stripped, so it never enters the octal branch and falls through to `parseInt("-010", 10)` → **-10** instead of **-8**.
- `-0xFF`: `startsWith("0x")` is now false, falls through to `parseInt("-0xFF", 10)` → **-0** instead of **-255**.
- `-0b101` → `-0` instead of `-5`; `+010` → `10` instead of `8`; `-1:30` → the sexagesimal branch computes with the embedded sign → `-30` instead of `-90`.

Plain decimals are unaffected because `parseInt` handles the sign itself, which presumably explains why the existing suite stays green — but signed non-decimal YAML 1.1 integers are broken. The default `load` schema (CORE) uses the untouched `parseYamlInteger$2`, so the test must opt into `YAML11_SCHEMA`.

